WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.680
All good things must come to an end.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:30.230
And so did the Pax Romana, a 200-year-long
period of time which represented the Roman

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:34.430
Empire at its most glorious and most powerful.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:38.570
It started with the formation of the empire
under Augustus and ended with the death of

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.610
Marcus Aurelius, the last of the so-called
Five Good Emperors.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.320
Marcus Aurelius earned the sobriquet “The
Philosopher King” because he personified

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.470
the concept devised by Plato of a ruler who
was a lover of wisdom and who applied the

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.460
ideas he learned in his exercise of power.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:05.000
Marcus Aurelius was a Stoic who believed that
reason and virtue led to eudaimonia, or blessedness.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.970
Despite this, his reign as emperor was far
from enlightened or peaceful.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.750
During his rule, Rome had to contend with
two major wars, a rebellion, and a plague

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.610
that ravaged his empire.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:23.560
Early Years &amp; Rise to Power

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:30.440
Marcus Aurelius was born on April 26, 121
AD, to Marcus Annius Verus III and Domitia

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.440
Lucilla.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.080
His name at birth may have also been Marcus
Annius Verus just like his father, grandfather,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.550
and great-grandfather but, to avoid confusion,
we’ll just call him Marcus Aurelius from

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:40.550
the start.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:43.210
His family was wealthy and relatively influential.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:47.080
According to Cassius Dio, one of our main
sources for the reign of Marcus Aurelius,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.940
his family was somehow related to Emperor
Hadrian.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:55.250
Aurelius’s father was a politician who held
the office of praetor, but he died young when

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.740
Marcus was just a child.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:02.690
We’re not sure on the exact date, but it
would have been around 124 AD while he was

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.690
still in office.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.140
Later, Marcus Aurelius wrote that, while he
did not remember much of his father, from

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.770
what little memory he had of him plus what
other people have said, he characterized his

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:18.030
father by “modesty and manliness,” traits
that he sought to emulate.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.640
As far as his mother was concerned, Aurelius
said that from her he learned “religious

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.980
piety, generosity, not only refraining from
wrongdoing but even from thoughts of it, simplicity

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.000
in diet, and to be far removed from the ways
of the rich.”

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.280
After his father’s death, Marcus Aurelius
and his sister, Annia Cornificia Faustina,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.420
were adopted by their paternal grandfather,
Marcus Annius Verus II, who had been made

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.900
patrician during the reign of Vespasian.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.880
His father had been a praetor and his grandfather
served three times as consul so, naturally,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.840
it was expected for Marcus Aurelius to follow
in their footsteps.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.690
In 127 AD, when he was just six years old,
he was enrolled in the equestrian order by

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.900
nomination of Emperor Hadrian himself.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.440
Other than that, we do not have much information
about the early years of the future emperor,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.460
apart from the names of some of his teachers
such as Diognetus and Alexander of Cotiaeum.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.960
Later on, he was also tutored by Apollonius
of Chalcedon, the man who introduced him to

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.140
Stoicism, the philosophy that deeply influenced
all aspects of his life.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.710
The story of Marcus Aurelius’s rise to power
is a bit convoluted, but I think we can get

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:27.710
there.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:30.620
He was born during the reign of Hadrian, who
had no children of his own.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:35.510
Therefore, when the emperor fell ill in 136
AD, he thought it would be a good idea to

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.510
appoint an heir.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:43.110
He adopted Lucius Ceionius Commodus who, as
emperor-in-waiting, took the name Lucius Aelius.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.600
However, this turned out to be an ill-fated
choice, as Aelius died at the start of 138

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.600
AD.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:54.170
Next up, Hadrian adopted Antoninus Pius as
his heir, who became emperor later that same

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.210
year after Hadrian died on July 10.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:02.569
As it happened, Antoninus was also Marcus
Aurelius’s uncle as he married Faustina,

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:03.780
his father’s sister.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.580
Hadrian’s adoption of Antoninus came with
a few conditions.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:11.680
In turn, Antoninus had to adopt the surviving
son of his predecessor, a seven-year-old boy

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:16.199
also named Lucius Ceionius Commodus, as well
as his own nephew, Marcus Aurelius.

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:21.590
All of a sudden, Aurelius was the eldest son
of the emperor, which meant that he was next

00:04:21.590 --> 00:04:22.940
in line for the throne.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.930
Some historians have speculated that this
had been Hadrian’s intention all along,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.120
although why the emperor granted so much favor
to Aurelius, we’re not really sure.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.780
Anyway, Antoninus was now the new Roman Emperor
and, to strengthen the bond with his newly

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:43.030
adopted son, he also had Marcus Aurelius marry
his natural born daughter, Faustina the Younger.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.220
The two of them would go on to have 13 children
together.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.460
Unsurprisingly, Marcus Aurelius’s position
as heir apparent brought with it a lot of

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.460
new promotions.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.470
He was first made consul in 140.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:58.050
He also became the head of the equestrian
order, he joined the colleges of priests,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:03.010
he took up residence in the imperial palace,
and was made quaestor to learn about the paperwork

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.340
and oration that were needed to rule Rome.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.500
The highest office he reached while Antoninus
was still emperor was praetorian prefect.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:16.120
At one point, he also changed his name to
Marcus Aelius Aurelius Verus Caesar, but we

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.070
will keep referring to him simply as Marcus
Aurelius for simplicity’s sake.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:25.150
Meanwhile, his adopted brother took on the
name Lucius Verus and followed a similar path,

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:27.070
serving both as quaestor and consul.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.150
One notable difference between them was their
physical prowess.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:35.310
Marcus Aurelius was “frail in body,” as
Cassius Dio put it, while Verus was “a vigorous

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:40.020
man of younger years” who was proficient
in hunting and wrestling, but took pleasure

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.980
in all athletic pursuits.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.120
Around 160 AD, Antoninus fell ill.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.160
He died the following year at his ancestral
home in Lorium, ending what was then the second-longest

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.430
reign of the Roman Empire after that of Augustus.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:56.590
A few days later, he was deified at the request
of his sons.

00:05:56.590 --> 00:06:01.380
In his book, Marcus Aurelius spoke very fondly
of his adoptive father, saying that no other

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.060
man had more influence on him as a youth.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:08.520
“The king is dead, long live the king,”
as the expression goes.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.820
Antoninus was gone and Rome needed an emperor.

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:16.390
The Senate was ready to confirm Marcus Aurelius
as the new sovereign, but something curious

00:06:16.390 --> 00:06:17.390
happened.

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:22.320
Marcus Aurelius refused to take office unless
his younger brother, Lucius Verus, was made

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.880
co-emperor alongside him.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:29.100
It was unusual, but the Senate accepted and
March 8, 161 AD, marked the first time that

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.810
the Roman Empire was ruled by two men.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:36.070
That being said, it was never in question
who the true leader of Rome was.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:40.410
Besides the fact that Marcus Aurelius was
the chosen heir, he was also the older sibling,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:45.100
he had experience helping Antoninus run the
empire, and he was the only one with the title

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.030
of Pontifex Maximus.

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:52.080
Verus obeyed Marcus “as a lieutenant obeys
a proconsul.”

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.380
You might think this is a setup for some kind
of conspiracy, or betrayal, or even assassination

00:06:58.380 --> 00:06:59.380
down the line.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:05.060
That’s usually how these situations unfolded
in ancient times, but we have here a rare

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.060
exception.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.940
Despite their different personalities, the
brothers stayed on good terms for the extent

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:14.920
of their joint rule and never did one of them
try to usurp the other.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:20.110
The War in Parthia

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:25.331
You might think that the rule of a calm, practical,
and amiable man would mark a time of peace

00:07:25.331 --> 00:07:27.680
for the Roman Empire, but that was not to
be.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:32.949
In fact, almost for his entire 19-year reign,
Marcus Aurelius was involved in one conflict

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:33.949
or another.

00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:37.990
First up was the Parthian Empire, a mighty
Middle Eastern faction that had been around

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.790
since the 3rd century BC.

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:42.850
This was not the first time that these two
powers clashed.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.639
It seemed that every time the Parthians wanted
to expand to the west, they eventually ran

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:49.550
into the dominion of Rome.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:53.889
This usually took the form of the Kingdom
of Armenia, a land that acted as a buffer

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:59.330
between the two empires who both wanted to
install an Armenian king who served their

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.330
best interests.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.690
The last time this happened was during the
rule of Trajan who we already covered here

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:04.690
on Biographics.

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:09.500
If you need a quick refresher on him, why
not check the video in the link below.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.850
That happened almost 50 years before Marcus
Aurelius became emperor and, in the end, Armenia

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:19.570
became a client state of Rome with its king
firmly under Roman control.

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:25.340
It seems that every few decades, a new Parthian
king gets the same idea - to remove Armenia

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.880
from under Roman influence and place it under
Parthian influence.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.320
In this case, it was King Vologases IV.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:38.740
In 161 AD, he marched into Armenia and deposed
King Sohaemus, replacing him with his own

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:40.190
son, Pacorus.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:44.630
The Romans were clearly taken by surprise
by this move and their initial reply was disastrous.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:50.500
There were two Roman provinces that bordered
the Kingdom of Armenia: Cappadocia and Syria.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:56.310
Marcus Sedatius Severianus, the Governor of
Cappadocia, decided to take matters into his

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:00.730
own hands, even though he was not supposed
to act without instructions from the emperor.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:05.139
Allegedly, an oracle convinced him that he
would overpower the Parthians so he took a

00:09:05.139 --> 00:09:07.689
legion and marched into Armenia.

00:09:07.689 --> 00:09:12.089
He reached Elegeia where his entire army was
surrounded and massacred.

00:09:12.089 --> 00:09:16.300
As for Severianus himself, he was either killed
along with his soldiers or he committed suicide

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.209
once he realized how badly he had been defeated.

00:09:19.209 --> 00:09:24.000
Seizing the upper hand, the Parthians then
invaded Syria and bested the Romans there,

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.000
as well.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.809
The first wave of the war went decidedly in
the Parthians’ favor and it didn’t look

00:09:28.809 --> 00:09:31.009
like things were going to get better for the
Romans.

00:09:31.009 --> 00:09:35.480
They were led by two emperors who were completely
inexperienced in military matters.

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:40.370
They also had threats to deal with in other
frontier provinces such as Britain and Germania

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:41.370
Superior.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:45.929
Moreover, the defeat and subsequent retreat
of the Roman forces in Syria created perfect

00:09:45.929 --> 00:09:47.470
conditions for a rebellion.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:52.810
Eventually, Marcus Aurelius decided that Lucius
Verus should go in person to guide the war

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:53.850
against Parthia.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:10:00.350
If anyone was expecting Verus to be a great,
inspiring commander, this was the moment that

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:01.910
did away with that notion.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:06.709
As it turned out, Verus was the type of leader
who preferred to pursue earthly delights and

00:10:06.709 --> 00:10:08.699
let others do the fighting for him.

00:10:08.699 --> 00:10:14.529
Allegedly, he spent most of the Parthian campaign
at a resort in Antioch where he feasted and

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:17.269
gambled daily, surrounded by his entourage.

00:10:17.269 --> 00:10:22.199
As it said in the Historia Augusta: “...while
legions were being slaughtered, while Syria

00:10:22.199 --> 00:10:29.550
medidated revolt, and the East was being devastated,
Verus was hunting in Apulia, travelling about

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:33.339
through Athens and Corinth accompanied by
orchestras and singers.”

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:38.100
At this time, we should also point out that
the veracity of the Historia Augusta is highly

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.269
uncertain so don’t take anything from it
as absolute truth.

00:10:42.269 --> 00:10:48.759
For his part, Cassius Dio confirmed that Verus
made his headquarters in Antioch and didn’t

00:10:48.759 --> 00:10:53.259
personally take part in any battles, but made
no mention of his party lifestyle.

00:10:53.259 --> 00:10:57.769
Whatever the truth, fortunately for Rome,
there were also a few capable generals who

00:10:57.769 --> 00:11:00.809
left with Verus and continued on their way
to Parthia.

00:11:00.809 --> 00:11:06.930
Some of the more important ones were Martius
Verus, Statius Priscus, and Avidius Cassius.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.060
Remember that last one as he will be important
later.

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:14.860
Martius Verus attacked Armenia and conquered
the capital of Artaxata.

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:19.439
He then restored Sohaemus to the throne, the
previous king who was subservient to Rome,

00:11:19.439 --> 00:11:22.910
and assumed the governorship of Cappadocia.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:28.230
Historian Cassius Dio gives most of the credit
for the eventual Roman victory to Avidius

00:11:28.230 --> 00:11:29.230
Cassius.

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:35.379
After King Vologases retreated back to Parthia,
he gave chase, and in 166 AD they sacked the

00:11:35.379 --> 00:11:39.830
cities of Seleucia and Ctesiphon, the latter
being the Parthian capital.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:44.259
Afterwards, Avidius made his way to Syria
where he eliminated any threat of a rebellion

00:11:44.259 --> 00:11:47.660
and was installed as the new governor of the
province.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:52.470
Later, Marcus Aurelius would grant him control
over all the Roman territories in Asia, a

00:11:52.470 --> 00:12:37.470
decision he would come

00:12:37.470 --> 00:13:20.819
to regret.

00:13:20.819 --> 00:13:37.490
The Antonine Plague

00:13:37.490 --> 00:13:41.809
Despite just winning a war, things soon went
from bad to worse for the Roman Empire.

00:13:41.809 --> 00:13:44.610
The soldiers returning home did not come alone.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:48.889
They brought with them a mysterious illness
that started one of the first known pandemics

00:13:48.889 --> 00:13:49.889
in history.

00:13:49.889 --> 00:13:55.059
It became known as the Antonine Plague, also
known as the Plague of Galen, named after

00:13:55.059 --> 00:13:59.430
the Greek physician who described the disease
and its symptoms in his treatise Methodus

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:00.430
Medendi.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:05.279
It caused fever, vomiting, swollen throat,
diarrhea, thirstiness, and violent coughing

00:14:05.279 --> 00:14:08.649
fits that produced a foul odor and skin rashes
all over the body.

00:14:08.649 --> 00:14:12.709
The illness lasted for about two weeks and
the ones fortunate enough to survive it became

00:14:12.709 --> 00:14:14.410
immune from further infection.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:17.779
The exact nature of the disease is still somewhat
of a mystery.

00:14:17.779 --> 00:14:21.529
Smallpox and measles were generally regarded
as the most likely culprits, both capable

00:14:21.529 --> 00:14:25.750
of ravaging a population that had no resistance
to them whatsoever.

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:29.569
In more recent times, the second candidate
has fallen out of favor and both scholars

00:14:29.569 --> 00:14:33.189
and doctors believe smallpox was the true
cause of the plague.

00:14:33.189 --> 00:14:37.100
They point to archaeological evidence that
depict people infected with the illness displaying

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:40.350
the pustules typically associated with smallpox.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:44.679
While the Romans picked up the plague while
campaigning in Parthia, the exact origins

00:14:44.679 --> 00:14:46.069
are shrouded in legend.

00:14:46.069 --> 00:14:49.610
There are multiple versions of the story,
but they all seem to suggest that the disease

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:55.059
was punishment from the gods because the Romans
allegedly broke an oath they made not to pillage

00:14:55.059 --> 00:14:57.120
the city of Seleucia.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.739
Trying to determine the impact of the pandemic
with accuracy is impossible.

00:15:00.739 --> 00:15:05.589
Some say it wasn’t as bad as everyone thinks
and that it demonstrated the resilience of

00:15:05.589 --> 00:15:09.779
Rome, while others believe it marked the beginning
of the fall of the Western Roman Empire.

00:15:09.779 --> 00:15:14.720
At its height, it killed thousands of people
a day as it spread throughout all the Roman

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:15.720
territories.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:20.540
It lasted until 180 AD and some believe that
the Plague of Cyprian which appeared 70 years

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:24.019
later was, in fact, another outbreak of the
same illness.

00:15:24.019 --> 00:15:28.549
Lastly, the Antonine Plague may have claimed
the life of Lucius Verus.

00:15:28.549 --> 00:15:33.779
While we don’t have details surrounding
his demise, Verus fell ill in 169 AD and died

00:15:33.779 --> 00:15:39.529
on January 23, leaving Marcus Aurelius as
the sole Emperor of Rome.

00:15:39.529 --> 00:15:44.649
The Marcomannic Wars

00:15:44.649 --> 00:15:49.769
The Antonine Plague was just one of the crises
that Marcus Aurelius had to contend with following

00:15:49.769 --> 00:15:51.559
the end of the Parthian War.

00:15:51.559 --> 00:15:56.859
The other one was barbarian invasions by Germanic
peoples along its northern frontier which

00:15:56.859 --> 00:15:59.939
was also known as Limes Germanicus.

00:15:59.939 --> 00:16:04.519
Such border conflicts were relatively common
for the Romans and represented an almost-constant

00:16:04.519 --> 00:16:06.730
threat to their outermost provinces.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:10.470
In fact, when the enemy was strong and determined
enough, they represented a threat to Rome

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:15.290
itself as just a few centuries later, the
heart of the empire was sacked multiple times

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.689
by invading Goths and Vandals.

00:16:17.689 --> 00:16:22.919
The situation was not as dire this time around,
but the Roman Empire was clearly at a disadvantage.

00:16:22.919 --> 00:16:26.749
It had just finished a war, meaning that its
coffers were getting empty and its people

00:16:26.749 --> 00:16:28.569
were growing weary of conflict.

00:16:28.569 --> 00:16:32.899
Most of its northern border was poorly defended
because the troops had been redirected to

00:16:32.899 --> 00:16:33.899
Parthia.

00:16:33.899 --> 00:16:37.799
And last, but certainly by no means least,
there was the plague to deal with.

00:16:37.799 --> 00:16:41.870
It certainly seemed like an opportune time
to attack, but scholars are still debating

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.309
over the exact motive for the invasion.

00:16:44.309 --> 00:16:49.009
Some believe that the most obvious answer
is true - the Germanic tribes simply sought

00:16:49.009 --> 00:16:51.889
to take advantage of the weakened state of
the Roman Empire.

00:16:51.889 --> 00:16:56.670
Others speculate that it might have been overpopulation
or another threat from the East which forced

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.351
them to expand into the West.

00:16:58.351 --> 00:17:02.309
The main belligerents (besides the Romans,
of course) were the Germanic Marcomanni, hence

00:17:02.309 --> 00:17:03.869
the name Marcomannic Wars.

00:17:03.869 --> 00:17:07.980
They were joined by another Germanic tribe
called the Quadi, and the Iazyges, who were

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:12.160
actually a Sarmatian people originating in
the steppes of Eurasia.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.620
They were assisted, at various points, by
other tribes as the Marcomannic Wars were

00:17:15.620 --> 00:17:20.160
a major conflict that lasted for almost two
decades and extended into the reign of Marcus

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:21.760
Aurelius’s successor.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.470
The leader of the opposing forces was Ballomar,
King of the Marcomanni.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:30.700
Cassius Dio first mentions him for acting
as mediator following some barbarian skirmishes

00:17:30.700 --> 00:17:32.040
into Roman territory.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:36.150
This happened in the years shortly before
the outbreak of the war and involved other

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:42.790
Germanic tribes such as the Longobards and
the Ubii on one side and Marcus Jallius Bassus,

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:46.450
then-governor of the Roman province of Pannonia
Inferior, on the other.

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:50.150
What exactly persuaded Ballomar to turn from
conciliator to aggressor, we don’t know,

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:55.610
but in 166 AD he united several tribes and
crossed the Danube to lead incursions into

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:56.910
Roman territory.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.100
Because of the other problems in the empire,
it wasn’t until 168 that Marcus Aurelius

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:00.440
was ready to respond.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:05.070
The Roman army marched to Pannonia and set
up a base at Aquileia.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.410
This time, both emperors were there to lead
the troops.

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:12.450
According to the Historia Augusta, many tribal
leaders immediately started retreating or

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:13.850
asking for pardons.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:18.370
For a while, it seemed like this conflict
would fizzle out as soon as it started, but

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:22.640
Marcus Aurelius was concerned that this was
all a ploy to stall for time.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:28.390
He wanted to keep pursuing the enemy, but
this was when Lucius Verus fell ill and died.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:32.890
The remaining emperor had to return to Rome
to make preparations for his brother’s funeral.

00:18:32.890 --> 00:18:36.530
Marcus Aurelius returned to the battlefield
later that same year, accompanied by his general

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.070
and son-in-law Claudius Pompeianus.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:44.140
A little sidenote here: Cassius Dio singled
out Pompeianus as the military commander who

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:49.720
distinguished himself the most during the
Marcomannic Wars, alongside another lieutenant

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:54.200
named Pertinax who would go on to become emperor
during one of the most tumultuous periods

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:57.050
in Roman history, but that’s a story for
another time.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.740
For now, we see the barbarian tribes gaining
the upper hand in the war.

00:19:00.740 --> 00:19:06.190
The Iazyges invaded the provinces of Moesia
and Dacia and killed the governor Claudius

00:19:06.190 --> 00:19:09.210
Fronto who served as general during the Parthian
War.

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:12.540
Other tribes went into Greece and destroyed
one of the most important religious sites

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:15.520
in the Roman Empire, the Temple of Eleusinian
Mysteries.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.850
But the Marcomanni were the most dangerous
of all.

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:24.150
They scored a decisive victory at Carnuntum
in 170 AD where they faced off against an

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:28.280
inexperienced Roman army and slain 20,000
soldiers.

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:32.970
They kept pushing until they reached Italy
itself, marking the first time in over 270

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:37.180
years that an enemy of Rome managed to penetrate
into the heart of the empire.

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:41.430
Marcus Aurelius realized that the Marcomanni
posed the greatest threat and considered them

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.240
a priority when launching a counter-offensive.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:48.630
Legions from other frontiers were rerouted
to fight Ballomar and this strategy proved

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:49.630
successful.

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:53.970
By the end of 171 AD, they had pushed the
Marcomanni out of Roman territory and continued

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.560
their pursuit across the Danube until the
Marcomanni were defeated.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.840
The other, smaller tribes began seeking audiences
with Marcus Aurelius.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:04.590
Some of them surrendered, others offered alliances.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:09.180
The Quadi agreed to a peace treaty, but then
broke it and went to war again.

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:14.710
Aurelius defeated them once more and installed
a pro-Roman king called Furtius, but the Quadi

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:18.040
deposed him and renewed hostilities yet again.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:22.010
The two sides met in battle and the Quadi
would have defeated the Romans if not for

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:25.280
divine intervention, according to Cassius
Dio, at least.

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:30.570
He says that one of Marcus Aurelius’s retinue,
an Egyptian magician called Arnuphis, prayed

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:32.520
to Mercury and summoned a storm.

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:37.140
While the Romans were reinvigorated by the
pouring rain, the Quadi were struck by lightning

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:38.750
and engulfed in flames.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.360
Nothing as dramatic happened with the Iazyges.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:47.380
They simply surrendered and their King Zanticus
agreed to the terms set by Marcus Aurelius.

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:51.760
The emperor’s triumphs are recorded on the
Column of Marcus Aurelius still located in

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:53.750
the Piazza Colonna in Rome.

00:20:53.750 --> 00:20:57.990
It was modeled on the triumphal column of
Trajan who celebrated his victories over the

00:20:57.990 --> 00:20:58.990
Dacians.

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:02.570
Also during his years in battle, Marcus Aurelius
started writing down his personal philosophies

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:06.990
which formed the basis for one of the most
influential works on Stoicism.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:11.830
Even today, his book titled Meditations is
still a popular seller, although the emperor

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:13.660
never intended it to be published.

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:17.830
Some argue that he wrote it for himself, others
that it was for his sons.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:21.240
Going back to the battle, this was only the
First Marcomannic War.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:24.810
It was the biggest of them all, but it was
followed by two more.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:29.390
It has been speculated that Marcus Aurelius
had grander ambitions and might have wanted

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:34.130
to eliminate the threat permanently during
the first war and establish new Roman provinces.

00:21:34.130 --> 00:21:38.440
However, he had little choice but to accept
quick victories and peace treaties.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:43.310
His attention was needed elsewhere as trouble
was brewing in Syria.

00:21:43.310 --> 00:21:49.070
A New Emperor Rises

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:53.160
Remember Avidius Cassius, the general from
the Parthian War who was in charge of Syria

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:54.580
and the surrounding provinces?

00:21:54.580 --> 00:21:59.870
Well, in 175 AD, he decided to rise against
Marcus Aurelius and declare himself the new

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:00.870
Emperor of Rome.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:04.270
While there is controversy surrounding his
motives, he did it because word had reached

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:07.360
him that Aurelius had died during the Marcomannic
War.

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:12.330
Even more controversially, historian Cassius
Dio pointed the finger at Faustina, the emperor’s

00:22:12.330 --> 00:22:15.100
wife, as being the one who engineered this
deception.

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:20.030
Allegedly, she also believed that her husband
would die in the war and feared that an outsider

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:22.690
would kill her son to take the throne by force.

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:27.520
Therefore, she preferred Cassius as emperor
because he would not take such drastic measures.

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:31.840
Anyway, Cassius eventually found out that
Marcus Aurelius was still alive but, by then,

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:33.090
it was too late to go back.

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:36.840
Well, actually, it might not have been if
we’re going by Dio again.

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:41.170
He portrayed Marcus Aurelius as one of the
most gracious Roman emperors in history because

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:45.150
he was not only willing to forgive the man
who was once one of his strongest allies,

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:49.620
but also said in a speech that if the soldiers,
Senate, and people of Rome preferred Cassius

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:53.230
over him, he would surrender his power without
struggle.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:57.310
Of course, this may have all simply been a
way of rallying the troops to his side.

00:22:57.310 --> 00:23:01.590
After all, what Marcus Aurelius actually did
was end the Marcomannic War as fast as possible

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.870
and head to Asia with his army.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:08.640
Meanwhile, Cassius was raising his own force,
but a battle between the two never took place.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.970
Just three months after the rebellion started,
General Cassius was assassinated by his own

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:15.750
men who were still loyal to the true emperor.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:20.020
His head was brought to Marcus Aurelius, but
he allegedly refused to see it and ordered

00:23:20.020 --> 00:23:21.020
to have it buried.

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:25.320
Whether or not Faustina was involved in the
plot remains uncertain, but she also died

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:26.640
that same year.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:31.370
Regardless of her intentions, her husband
remained devoted to her and deified her and

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:32.810
gave her a lavish funeral.

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:37.000
With the rebellion over, Marcus Aurelius was
finally able to return to Rome where he had

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:38.820
his son, Commodus, named co-emperor.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:44.320
Initially, he intended two of his sons to
rule the empire as he did with Lucius Verus,

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:48.240
but his other heir, Annius Verus, died when
he was a boy.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:53.000
Peace was brief as the emperor had to leave
in 177 to fight in the Second Marcomannic

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.000
War.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:59.700
He would never return to Rome again as he
died on March 17, 180, at Vindobona, modern

00:23:59.700 --> 00:24:01.010
day Vienna.

00:24:01.010 --> 00:24:06.590
He was 58 years old and, although he was seemingly
a man who cherished wisdom and eudaimonia,

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:11.230
he was forced to spend almost his entire reign
plunged into war and chaos.

00:24:11.230 --> 00:24:15.610
He was followed by his son Commodus who was
the antithesis of his father - cruel, vain,

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:19.400
merciless, debauched, inept, and a complete
megalomaniac.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.380
Never again would mighty Rome reach the same
heights.

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:26.720
As Cassius Dio put it, after the death of
Marcus Aurelius, the empire descended “from

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:41.899
a kingdom of gold to one of iron and rust.”

